1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,600
Your vendor emails:
"We have changed banks.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,600
Please send this month's payment
to our new account."

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,800
Same name, same invoice. Pay it, and
the money goes to whoever sent it.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:14,300
Business email compromise hit about
three in four organizations in 2025.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,800
Of the companies hit by payments fraud,
only three in ten got most of it back.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,800
One in five got nothing. Losses reported
to the FBI: over three billion dollars.

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:27,000
The old fix still works: call the vendor
back on the number you already have.

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,800
The scammer controls the email.
Not the vendor's phone.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,600
So we automated the call.

10
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:39,600
This is Rice Robotics' accounts payable.
I'm sending the scam email now,

11
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,600
through the same code path
a real email takes.

12
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,600
Claude reads it and flags the bank change,
and the rules flag it too.

13
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:55,000
Both Acme bills, twenty-four thousand
eight hundred dollars, go on hold.

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,600
Ramp is a mock here,
built against Ramp's Developer API.

15
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:05,400
It never dials a number from the email.

16
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,800
It calls the one on Acme's W-9 from
March 2025, the number we already had.

17
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:19,600
Acme says no. The case is blocked,
and the twenty-four eight stays on hold.

18
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:26,600
Only a signed-in person can release money,
by holding this button. Never the model.

19
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:33,600
Every step lands in one append-only log, and
the audit page rebuilds every case from it.

20
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:40,600
Voicemail: the agent says nothing about
the request, and a person gets a task.

21
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:46,600
"I think so?" isn't a yes.
Still held, and a person follows up.

22
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:54,600
Someone says yes, but can't give the right
digits? Held, and sent to a person.

23
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:01,600
And when every check passes,
the money still waits for a person.

24
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:06,600
One Next.js app.
Email arrives by a signed webhook,

25
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,600
Claude returns typed JSON,
and Retell places the call.

26
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,600
The number in the email
never reaches the dialer.

27
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:19,800
The models only report. Plain TypeScript
decides: an unhandled outcome won't compile.

28
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:24,800
"Confirmed" needs the right digits, no red
flags, and a second reader that agrees.

29
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:29,600
Even then, only a signed-in person
releases the money.

30
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:35,600
On our twenty hand-built emails, the live model
got twenty of twenty, with no false alarms.

31
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,600
With the model switched off, the rules
alone still hold every bank change.

32
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:46,000
We check the policy exhaustively: almost
twenty-one million cases, zero unsafe releases.

33
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:51,600
A seeded simulator loses, repeats and
forges webhooks. It has found two real bugs.

34
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:56,600
And the audit log is sealed, entry by entry,
so a changed record shows up.

35
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:00,800
The old way depends on someone
remembering to make a phone call.

36
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,800
CounterCall makes it, every time.
